00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:08.557
Broker?

00:12:08.557 --> 00:12:10.140
SANDEEP DINESH: So
Open Service Broker

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:14.160
is an idea of using
services outside

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:15.690
of a cloud native cluster--

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:17.550
natively in that cluster, right.

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:20.160
So let's say you're
in a Kubernetes world

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.686
and you want to consume
a service from your cloud

00:12:22.686 --> 00:12:24.060
provider, just
like Google Cloud,

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:26.130
and maybe like
Pub/Sub or Spanner.

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:27.780
You can connect to
it without having

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:30.362
to go to the Google Cloud
page or something like that

00:12:30.362 --> 00:12:31.070
and provision it.

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:33.510
You can connect to it natively
in your Kubernetes cluster.

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:33.960
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:34.880
MARK MANDEL: How does that work?

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:37.046
SANDEEP DINESH: Yeah, so
basically there's a service

00:12:37.046 --> 00:12:38.610
catalog and the broker itself.

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:41.616
And the catalog will have a list
of all the different services

00:12:41.616 --> 00:12:42.990
that are available
to you, right.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:46.350
So let's say you want
to browse the catalog.

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:49.590
You see oh, this Pub/Sub thing
looks really cool, great.

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:51.510
And then with the
broker, you can actually

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:54.030
provision a Pub/Sub
instance and then

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:56.960
connect to it without having
to actually run any G Cloud

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:58.410
commands or anything like that.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:12:59.610
MELANIE WARRICK: And we
were talking a little bit

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:01.151
before we started
